WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:03.480
Hi there, Steve Kaufmann here again
to talk about language learning.

00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:11.100
And I wanted to share a secret with you,
uh, something that I consider to be a very

00:00:11.105 --> 00:00:16.710
important secret when it comes to language
learning and that is that it takes a

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:20.070
long time to become fluent in a language.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.760
To even become comfortable in
a language takes a long time.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:26.670
And I wanted to mention
this for a couple of reason.

00:00:27.195 --> 00:00:34.155
First of all, because, uh, when I
discussed ChatGPT I pointed out that one

00:00:34.155 --> 00:00:39.885
of the useful things that can be done
with ChatGPT when it comes to language

00:00:39.885 --> 00:00:46.935
learning is to create customized learning
content so that you can ask ChatGPT to

00:00:46.935 --> 00:00:53.415
create a story for you to simulate a
dialogue to, you know, explain aspects

00:00:53.415 --> 00:00:55.905
of the grammar in the target language.

00:00:55.905 --> 00:01:01.725
All of this is content, suitable
content, maybe comprehensible content.

00:01:02.295 --> 00:01:08.085
However, I, I kind of wanted to add
that, you know, the question is,

00:01:08.385 --> 00:01:13.495
how much time are you gonna spend
creating content with ChatGPT?

00:01:13.965 --> 00:01:18.975
Um, there is a lot of content
available, particularly for beginners.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:24.420
There is a lot of genuine,
authentic content, but there is

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.070
a lack of intermediate content.

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:29.580
It takes time to find
good intermediate content.

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:35.530
Maybe ChatGPT can be used as a way
to bridge that gap, but ultimately

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.429
you need to consume so much content.

00:01:39.315 --> 00:01:42.585
You need to consume authentic,
interesting content.

00:01:42.585 --> 00:01:48.044
You need to read so much, listen
to so much that I just didn't wanna

00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:53.175
leave people with the impression that
ChatGPT was some kind of a shortcut to

00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:57.824
learning the language we do and, and
ultimately you have to engage in the

00:01:57.824 --> 00:02:02.985
sort of language learning activities
of listening, reading, writing, speak.

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:09.329
And so ChatGPT maybe has, you
know, uh, a function to play there.

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:13.470
And that's gonna vary from person
to person, but it's no magic bullet.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:16.950
You often hear people say, you know,
fluent in three months, fluent in

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:20.820
five days, uh, master the language
in whatever, how many months.

00:02:21.705 --> 00:02:25.065
Uh, you know, books that they
sell you, you're gonna be at, you

00:02:25.065 --> 00:02:27.734
know, B2 on the European scale
when you complete this book.

00:02:27.734 --> 00:02:29.085
All of that is not true.

00:02:29.685 --> 00:02:33.585
Uh, you, you get a smattering, you
get an introduction to the language.

00:02:33.765 --> 00:02:36.825
There is even one person, I think it
was Tim Ferris, who said, you know,

00:02:36.825 --> 00:02:42.585
four hours to learn, although not
master, but to learn any language.

00:02:42.765 --> 00:02:46.025
Well, yeah, you can learn a
few things about a language.

00:02:46.025 --> 00:02:51.644
You can ask ChatGPT how the language is
structured and you can get an overview,

00:02:51.945 --> 00:02:57.015
but eventually to learn that language,
to be comfortable, to understand,

00:02:57.285 --> 00:03:00.255
to understand what you're listening
to, to be able to use the language.

00:03:00.524 --> 00:03:04.515
You have to consume a lot of the
language, you have to acquire a

00:03:04.519 --> 00:03:07.815
lot of words, you have to hear
these words in different contexts.

00:03:08.265 --> 00:03:12.135
And you know, in that regard, and,
and this is the other reason why

00:03:12.135 --> 00:03:15.785
I'm making this video, so I may
have mentioned that I'm going to

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:18.959
Sweden, uh, sort of end of May.

00:03:19.109 --> 00:03:23.130
Therefore, I'm gonna spend three, four
days in Denmark, followed up by 10 days

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:26.399
in Poland in May, or sorry, in early June.

00:03:26.579 --> 00:03:30.089
So in preparation for that,
I've been first doing Danish,

00:03:30.095 --> 00:03:31.079
then I'm gonna do Polish.

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:35.415
So I've been uh, reading and
listening to this audiobook.

00:03:35.415 --> 00:03:38.865
Very interesting audiobook
and ebook about the history of

00:03:38.865 --> 00:03:41.025
Denmark learning a bunch of stuff.

00:03:41.235 --> 00:03:45.315
I mean, every country writes
history from its point of view.

00:03:45.435 --> 00:03:49.575
Denmark is the centre of the world
for history, insofar as Denmark is

00:03:49.575 --> 00:03:54.765
concerned, just as Iran is the centre for
history, insofar as Iran is concerned,

00:03:54.975 --> 00:03:59.000
or Great Britain is the centre or
North America or the United States

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:00.640
is the center of China is the centre.

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:03.760
Wherever people write history,
they put themselves in the centre.

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:08.029
And so it's very interesting to see world
history from different perspectives.

00:04:08.089 --> 00:04:13.010
And that's what I'm enjoying in this
history of Denmark, which inevitably

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:15.920
involves other countries besides Denmark.

00:04:16.159 --> 00:04:22.610
And because I speak Swedish, a lot of
the vocabulary in Danish is already

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:24.890
familiar or recognizable to me.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:28.260
Uh, but I have difficulty
understanding Danish.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:31.500
But nevertheless, of course, I
can go through this book, both the

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:34.620
audiobook and the ebook quite quickly.

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:38.760
And so if you look at my statistics
over the last two weeks, because it's

00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:42.270
just in the last two weeks that I
started doing this, you'll see how

00:04:42.275 --> 00:04:47.469
much I have read in Danish, which
I've done essentially all on LingQ.

00:04:47.849 --> 00:04:50.760
I'm going from memory, but
I think it's over 25,000.

00:04:52.245 --> 00:04:55.675
The number of LingQs I have created,
which I think is five or 6,000 LingQs.

00:04:55.695 --> 00:05:00.375
So that's actually clicking on words that
I didn't know in order to make them known.

00:05:01.094 --> 00:05:05.415
Or of course, if I turn the page, I get
credit for words that I didn't look up.

00:05:05.415 --> 00:05:08.145
So it's not all words that I have
clicked, but it's words that I

00:05:08.150 --> 00:05:10.315
have added to my known words total.

00:05:10.895 --> 00:05:12.585
So that's a lot of words.

00:05:12.615 --> 00:05:17.910
In two weeks, if I look at my experience
in say, Persian then you'll see that

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:22.500
since I began with Persian quite a while
ago, just how many words I have read

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:24.660
and how many LingQs I have created.

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:29.130
What's not in these statistics is how
many hours I have listened, because

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:33.240
at least in the case of Danish, I
downloaded the audiobook, put it into

00:05:33.245 --> 00:05:35.490
iTunes, and I'm listening on iTunes.

00:05:35.495 --> 00:05:39.210
So I have listened a lot, but
because I didn't import the audio

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:43.200
files into LingQ, I have no record
of how many hours I have listened.

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:44.490
But it's quite a lot.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:49.409
Uh, if I look at some of the other
languages that I have on LingQ, even in a

00:05:49.409 --> 00:05:53.219
language like Czech, I mean, I have lists.

00:05:53.219 --> 00:05:55.200
I think I've read a
million and a half words.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:57.270
Um, yeah.

00:05:57.270 --> 00:06:00.330
And every time you're reading, of
course it's easier to understand

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:03.860
when you read because you look
things up, but I'm enjoying the

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:05.490
time that I'm spending reading.

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:07.650
I'm en I enjoyed reading
about Czech history.

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:09.630
I enjoy reading about Danish.

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:15.330
Uh, so it's not as if I'm in a
hurry to get out of this activity.

00:06:15.335 --> 00:06:16.260
I am enjoying it.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:22.530
I will enjoy doing the same with Polish
history, but I know from experience

00:06:22.534 --> 00:06:27.000
that even when I finish this Danish
history book, I will still be a

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:30.270
long way from mastery or fluency.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.840
Uh, I may have to read and
listen to a number of books.

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:35.970
I'll have to speak to people a lot.

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:39.330
Maybe I'll have the opportunity
to do that when I'm in Denmark.

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:44.550
Certainly, my comprehension has
improved, my vocabulary has increased.

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:46.170
I will be more comfortable.

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:48.990
I may not be able to speak Danish.

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:51.660
I'll probably reply more
in Swedish than in Danish.

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:54.370
But if I understand, I'm doing fine.

00:06:54.549 --> 00:06:59.859
Similarly, in Poland, uh, if I understand
well, but I reply in half Ukrainian,

00:06:59.859 --> 00:07:02.109
half Polish, it's not gonna matter.

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:05.409
Uh, I'm not there to prove anything.

00:07:05.409 --> 00:07:10.240
It's similar to someone who, uh,
you know, speaks Spanish and learns

00:07:10.245 --> 00:07:14.169
Italian and acquires vocabulary very
quickly and reads a lot in Italian.

00:07:14.349 --> 00:07:17.260
Listens to Italian, but still,
when you go to Italy, you'll be

00:07:17.260 --> 00:07:20.870
speaking more Spanish than Italian
and gradually you will shift over.

00:07:21.170 --> 00:07:25.545
So just a secret here:
language learning takes time.

00:07:25.755 --> 00:07:29.925
So it's important that you
find ways to enjoy the time you

00:07:29.925 --> 00:07:31.635
spend learning the language.

00:07:31.725 --> 00:07:35.475
That way, the process
itself becomes the reward.

00:07:36.195 --> 00:07:38.325
Thank you for listening, and
I'll leave you a couple of

00:07:38.325 --> 00:07:42.135
videos that cover a similar topic
that I've done over the years.

